of Education, 400715, Chongqing, China. xlei@swu.edu.cn.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><GrantID>32471095</GrantID><Agency>National Natural Science Foundation of China</Agency><Country/></Grant></GrantList><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>04</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>United States</Country><MedlineTA>Brain Topogr</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>8903034</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0896-0267</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Functional connectivity</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Sleep quality</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Social exclusion</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">fMRI</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>Declarations. Competing interests: The authors declare no competing interests. Ethics approval: This study was part of the Behavioral Brain Research Project of Chinese Personality (BBP) with participants from Southwest University in Chongqing, China and was approved by the Southwest University Ethics Committee (H20059). All participants provided written informed consent after receiving a detailed explanation of the study. They were informed that they could withdraw from the study at any stage if they felt uncomfortable. Author use of AI Tools Statement: The authors did not use generative AI technologies for preparation of this work.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2025</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>9</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>2</Month><Day>23</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>4</Day><Hour>15</Hour><Minute>49</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate 