ci. Bull. 37, 1&#x2013;14. doi: 10.1007/s12264-020-00553-z</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1007/s12264-020-00553-z</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC7811973</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">32785834</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zarate C. A., Payne J. L., Quiroz J., Sporn J., Denicoff K. K., Luckenbaugh D., et al. (2004). An open-label trial of riluzole in patients with treatment-resistant major depression. Am. J. Psychiatry 161, 171&#x2013;174. doi: 10.1176/appi.ajp.161.1.171</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1176/appi.ajp.161.1.171</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">14702270</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhang G.-M., Wu H.-Y., Cui W.-Q., Peng W., et al. (2022). Multi-level variations of lateral habenula in depression: a comprehensive review of current evidence. Front. Psychiatry 13:1043846. doi: 10.3389/fpsyt.2022.1043846</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3389/fpsyt.2022.1043846</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC9649931</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">36386995</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhang Y.-m., Qi Y.-b., Gao Y.-n., Chen W.-g., Zhou T., Zang Y., et al. (2023). Astrocyte metabolism and signaling pathways in the CNS. Front. Neurosci. 17:1217451. doi: 10.3389/fnins.2023.1217451</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3389/fnins.2023.1217451</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC10507181</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">37732313</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">41859329</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>20</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>20</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic-eCollection"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">2168-8184</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>18</Volume><Issue>3</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Mar</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Cureus</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Cureus</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Acute Reversible Convergent Strabismus Following Accidental Lorazepam Ingestion in a Child: A Rare Ocular Manifestation of Benzodiazepine Toxicity.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>e105405</StartPage><MedlinePgn>e105405</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">e105405</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.7759/cureus.105405</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Benzodiazepine intoxication in children commonly manifests with central nervous system depression, hypotonia, and ataxia. Ocular motor abnormalities are uncommon and may mimic serious neurological disorders, frequently prompting urgent neuroimaging. We report the case of an eight-year-old boy who presented with sudden-onset convergent strabismus approximately 30 minutes after accidental ingestion of 10 mg of lorazepam. On admission, he was hemodynamically stable with a Glasgow Coma Scale score of 15/15. Neurological examination was unremarkable except for acute esotropia. Brain computed tomography revealed no abnormalities, and urine toxicology screening was positive for benzodiazepines. The patient was managed conservatively with close clinical observation without administration of flumazenil. Complete spontaneous resolution of the ocular deviation occurred within 12 hours. This case highlights that isolated acute strabismus may represent a rare but reversible manifestation of benzodiazepine intoxication in children. Awareness of this presentation may help clinicians consider toxicological causes in the differential diagnosis and avoid unnecessary invasive investigations.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2026, El Athmani et al.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>El Athmani</LastName><ForeName>Omayma</ForeName><Initials>O</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Pediatrics, Children's Hospital, University Hospital Center Ibn Sina, Faculty of Medicine and Pharmacy, Mohammed V University, Rabat, MAR.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Debono</LastName><ForeName>Leila</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Pediatric Emergency Medicine, Children's Hospital, University Hospital Center Ibn Sina, Faculty of Medicine and Pharmacy, Mohammed V University, Rabat, MAR.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Jeddi</LastName><ForeName>Youssef</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Pediatric Emergency Medicine, Children's Hospital, University Hospital Center Ibn Sina, Faculty of Medicine and Pharmacy, Mohammed V University, Rabat, MAR.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Mekaoui</LastName><ForeName>Nour</ForeName><Initials>N</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Pediatric Emergency Medicine, Children's Hospital, University Hospital Center Ibn Sina, Faculty of Medicine and Pharmacy, Mohammed V University, Rabat, MAR.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Karboubi</LastName><ForeName>Lamya</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Pediatric Emergency Medicine, Children's Hospital, University Hospital Center Ibn Sina, Faculty of Medicine and Pharmacy, Mohammed V University, Rabat, MAR.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D002363">Case Reports</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>17</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>United States</Country><MedlineTA>Cureus</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>101596737</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>2168-8184</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">acute strabismus</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">benzodiazepine intoxication</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">esotropia</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">lorazepam poisoning</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">pediatric poisoning</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">toxicology</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>Human subjects: Informed consent for treatment and open access publication was obtained or waived by all participants in this study. Not applicable issued approval Not applicable. Written informed consent for publication was obtained from the patient&#x2019;s parents. Conflicts of interest: In compliance with the ICMJE uniform disclosure form, all authors declare the following: Payment/services info: All authors have declared that no financial support was received from any organization for the submitted work. Financial relationships: All authors have declared that they have no financial relationships at present or within the previous three years with any organizations that might have an interest in the submitted work. Other relationships: All authors have declared that there are no other relationships or activities that could appear to have influenced the submitted work.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>17</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>20</Day><Hour>7</Hour><Minute>11</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>20</Day><Hour>7</Hour><Minute>10</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>20</Day><Hour>4</Hour><Minute>53</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pmc-release"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>17</Day></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>epublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">41859329</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC12995505</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.7759/cureus.105405</ArticleId></ArticleIdList><ReferenceList><Reference><Citation>Benzodiazepine poisoning. Clinical and pharmacological considerations and treatment. Gaudreault P, Guay J, Thivierge RL, Verdy I. Drug Saf. 1991;6:247&#x2013;265.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">1888441</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Benzodiazepines: their use either as essential medicines or as toxics substances. Sanabria E, Cuenca RE, Esteso M&#xc1;, Maldonado M. Toxics. 2021;9</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC7912725</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">33535485</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Adverse events associated with flumazenil treatment for the management of suspected benzodiazepine intoxication--a systematic review with meta-analyses of randomised trials. Penninga EI, Graudal N, Ladekarl MB, J&#xfc;rgens G. Basic Clin Pharmacol Toxicol. 2016;118:37&#x2013;44.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">26096314</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>A clinical trial of escalating doses of flumazenil for reversal of suspected benzodiazepine overdose in the emergency department. Spivey WH, Fligner DJ, Rappaport SA, et al. Ann Emerg Med. 1993;22:1813&#x2013;1821.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">8239101</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Management of benzodiazepine overdose. H&#xf6;jer J. CNS Drugs. 1994;2:42&#x2013;55.</Citation></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="Publisher" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">41857525</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>20</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1471-244X</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Mar</Month><Day>19</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>BMC psychiatry</Title><ISOAbbreviation>BMC Psychiatry</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>High-gamma tACS may regulate brain network connectivity to alleviate symptoms in female adolescent non-suicidal self-injury: a preliminary TMS-EEG pilot study.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1186/s12888-026-07978-2</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore the efficacy and potential neurophysiological mechanisms of 77.5&#xa0;Hz transcranial alternating current stimulation (tACS) in the treatment of female adolescent non-suicidal self-injury (NSSI).</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Six female NSSI patients received 21 days of 77.5&#xa0;Hz, 15&#xa0;mA tACS treatments. Neuropsychological scales were assessed at baseline (W0), after treatment (W3), and 4-week (W7) and 8-week (W11) follow-ups. Transcranial magnetic stimulation with EEG evaluated changes in source-level brain activity and phase-synchronous functional connectivity. Mixed repeated-measures ANOVA with Bonferroni correction (p&#x2009;&lt;&#x2009;0.017) was used for behavioral data analysis to correct for multiple comparisons. Effect sizes (Cohen's d) was reported for all statistical results.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Significant improvements were observed in depressive symptoms and self-injury behaviors after treatment (OSIC: W7: P&#x2009;=&#x2009;0.009, Cohen's d=-1.682; HAMD-24: W3: P&#x2009;=&#x2009;0.006, Cohen's d=-1.892; W7: P&#x2009;=&#x2009;0.001, Cohen's d=-2.839; W11: P&#x2009;=&#x2009;0.001, Cohen's d=-2.738; all P&#x2009;&lt;&#x2009;0.017). Electrophysiological analysis revealed that 77.5&#xa0;Hz tACS might decrease Default network activity, increased Limbic, SalVAttn and Control network activity, and enhanced the functional connectivity in high-gamma band between Control and SalVAttn/Default network. A positive correlation was found between increased C100 activity in the SalVAttn (left frontal-insula) region and reduced HAMD-24 scores (R&#x2009;=&#x2009;0.826, p&#x2009;=&#x2009;0.043), this correlation analysis was based on a small sample size (n&#x2009;=&#x2009;6), and the correlation coefficient was unstable, with results only for preliminary exploratory reference.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">77.5&#xa0;Hz tACS may alleviate NSSI symptoms in female adolescents potentially by regulating brain activity and functional connectivity in emotional-control networks.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026. The Author(s).</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hao</LastName><ForeName>Wensi</ForeName><Initials>W</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Neurology, Xuanwu Hospital, Capital Medical University, No. 45 Changchun Street, Beijing, 100053, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Beijing Key Laboratory of Neuromodulation, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ding</LastName><ForeName>Xuemeng</ForeName><Initials>X</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Neuropsychiatric Treatment Center, Hebei Hospital of Xuanwu Hospital, Shijiazhuang, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ren</LastName><ForeName>Kenan</ForeName><Initials>K</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Neuropsychiatric Treatment Center, Hebei Hospital of Xuanwu Hospital, Shijiazhuang, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Cui</LastName><ForeName>Chang</ForeName><Initials>C</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Neuropsychiatric Treatment Center, Hebei Hospital of Xuanwu Hospital, Shijiazhuang, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhang</LastName><ForeName>Yuqing</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Neuropsychiatric Treatment Center, Hebei Hospital of Xuanwu Hospital, Shijiazhuang, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>He</LastName><ForeName>Jiahui</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Neuropsychiatric Treatment Center, Hebei Hospital of Xuanwu Hospital, Shijiazhuang, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Guo</LastName><ForeName>Xin</ForeName><Initials>X</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Neuropsychiatric Treatment Center, Hebei Hospital of Xuanwu Hospital, Shijiazhuang, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wang</LastName><ForeName>Yuping</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Neurology, Xuanwu Hospital, Capital Medical University, No. 45 Changchun Street, Beijing, 100053, China. wangyuping01@sina.cn.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Beijing Key Laboratory of Neuromodulation, Beijing, China. wangyuping01@sina.cn.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Neuropsychiatric Treatment Center, Hebei Hospital of Xuanwu Hospital, Shijiazhuang, China. wangyuping01@sina.cn.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ju</LastName><ForeName>Haiyan</ForeName><Initials>H</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Neuropsychiatric Treatment Center, Hebei Hospital of Xuanwu Hospital, Shijiazhuang, China. 337269479@qq.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lin</LastName><ForeName>Yicong</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Neurology, Xuanwu Hospital, Capital Medical University, No. 45 Changchun Street, Beijing, 100053, China. linyc_1@163.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Beijing Key Laboratory of Neuromodulation, Beijing, China. linyc_1@163.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><GrantID>2021YFC2501400</GrantID><Agency>National Key R&amp;D Program of China</Agency><Country/></Grant><Grant><GrantID>2023YFC2412003</GrantID><Agency>National Key R&amp;D Program of China</Agency><Country/></Grant></GrantList><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>19</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>England</Country><MedlineTA>BMC Psychiatry</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>100968559</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>1471-244X</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Brain network</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Functional connectivity</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Non-suicidal self-injury</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">TMS-EEG</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Transcranial alternating current stimulation</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>Declarations. Ethics approval and consent to participate: This pilot study was conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinkiand approved by the Ethics Committee of Hebei Hospital of Xuanwu Hospital, Capital Medical University [Approval Number: LYS(2024) 164] in October 2024, and written informed consent was obtained from the legal guardians of all patients prior to enrollment. Patients recruitment for this pilot study took place from December 2024 to January 2025. This pilot study serves as a preliminary feasibility investigation for a larger randomized controlled trial (RCT). The protocol for the main RCT has been registered with the Chinese Clinical Trial Registry (ChiCTR) (ChiCTR2600118169) in February 2026. Consent for publication: All data were analyzed in an anonymized and aggregated manner. Written informed consent was obtained from the legal guardians of minors for the publication of any potentially identifiable data or images included in this manuscript. Competing interests: The authors declare no competing interests.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2025</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>24</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>10</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>20</Day><Hour>7</Hour><Minute>10</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>20</Day><Hour>7</Hour><Minute>10</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>20</Day><Hour>0</Hour><Minute>34</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>aheadofprint</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">41857525</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1186/s12888-026-07978-2</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">10.1186/s12888-026-07978-2</ArticleId></ArticleIdList><ReferenceList><Reference><Citation>McCarron RM, Shapiro B, Rawles J, Luo J, Depression. Ann Intern Med. 2021;174(5):ITC65&#x2013;80. https://doi.org/10.7326/AITC202105180.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Boxer P. Variations in risk and treatment factors among adolescents engaging in different types of deliberate self-harm in an inpatient sample. J Clin Child Adolesc Psychol. 2010;39(4):470&#x2013;80. https://doi.org/10.1080/15374416.2010.486302.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Swannell SV, Martin GE, Page A, Hasking P, St John NJ. Prevalence of nonsuicidal self-injury in nonclinical samples: systematic review, meta-analysis and meta-regression. Suicide Life Threat Behav. 2014;44(3):273&#x2013;303. https://doi.org/10.1111/sltb.12070.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ho TC, Walker JC, Teresi GI, Kulla A, Kirshenbaum JS, Gifuni AJ, et al. Default mode and salience network alterations in suicidal and non-suicidal self-injurious thoughts and b