pairments via a <i>B. pseudolongum</i>-propionic acid-FFAR3 axis. iMeta
4:e70006. doi: 10.1002/imt2.70006, 
</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1002/imt2.70006</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC11995186</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">40236783</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhao T., Ren Y., Lu H., Kong Y. (2025). Decision level scheme for fusing multiomics and histology slide images using deep neural network for tumor prognosis prediction. Sci. Rep. 15:25479. doi: 10.1038/s41598-025-09869-0,</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/s41598-025-09869-0</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC12264071</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">40664732</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhao X., Yu J., Xu B., Xu Z., Lei X., Han S., et al. (2025). Gut-derived bacterial vesicles carrying lipopolysaccharide promote microglia-mediated synaptic pruning. Alzheimers Dement. 21:e70331. doi: 10.1002/alz.70331,</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1002/alz.70331</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC12307132</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">40731189</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhao H., Zhou X., Song Y., Zhao W., Sun Z., Zhu J., et al. (2025). Multi-omics analyses identify gut microbiota-fecal metabolites-brain-cognition pathways in the Alzheimer's disease continuum. Alzheimer's Res. Ther. 17:36. doi: 10.1186/s13195-025-01683-0,</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1186/s13195-025-01683-0</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC11786436</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">39893498</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhu J., Wang C., Qian Y., Cai H., Zhang S., Zhang C., et al. (2022). Multimodal neuroimaging fusion biomarkers mediate the association between gut microbiota and cognition. Prog. Neuro-Psychopharmacol. Biol. Psychiatry 113:110468. doi: 10.1016/j.pnpbp.2021.110468,</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.pnpbp.2021.110468</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">34736997</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhuo B., Zheng D., Cai M., Wang C., Zhang S., Zhang Z., et al. (2023). Mediation effect of brain volume on the relationship between peripheral inflammation and cognitive decline. J. Alzheimer's Dis 95, 523&#x2013;533. doi: 10.3233/jad-230253,</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3233/jad-230253</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">37545239</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="Publisher" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">41858232</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>20</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1399-0004</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Mar</Month><Day>20</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Clinical genetics</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Clin Genet</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Expanding the Phenotypic Spectrum of the Recurrent De Novo FBXO31 p.Asp334Asn Variant: Evidence for a Novel Neurodevelopmental Disorder (Kruer Syndrome).</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/cge.70166</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Biallelic loss-of-function variants in FBXO31 cause autosomal-recessive intellectual disability. A recurrent de novo variant, c.1000G&gt;A(p.Asp334Asn), has been described in association with an autosomal-dominant phenotype. To refine this phenotype and its clinical implications, we re-evaluated three published cases and ascertained four additional probands via advocacy networks, GeneMatcher, and clinician referral. Phenotyping included neurologic, behavioral, and dysmorphology assessment. All seven individuals carried the recurrent de novo FBXO31 p.Asp334Asn variant. A core neurodevelopmental profile was observed and included cerebral palsy (mixed hypotonia, spasticity, and dystonia), global developmental delay/intellectual disability, and speech impairment. Neuropsychiatric features were sometimes prominent and included attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder, anxiety, stereotypies, autistic features, and behavioral dysregulation. Neuroimaging often showed a hypoplastic corpus callosum and posterior-predominant white-matter changes. In one individual, gray matter heterotopias were also observed. A subtle but consistent facial gestalt was noted. Recurrent FBXO31 p.Asp334Asn variants lead to a recognizable neurodevelopmental syndrome. Based on our findings, we recommend including FBXO31 in diagnostic algorithms for cerebral palsy and neurodevelopmental disorders. We propose the descriptive term "autosomal dominant FBXO31-associated neurodevelopmental disorder," and-consistent with the validating laboratory and with support from the FBXO31 Foundation-propose the eponym "Kruer syndrome."</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026 John Wiley &amp; Sons A/S. Published by John Wiley &amp; Sons Ltd.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Galaz-Montoya</LastName><ForeName>Carolina I</ForeName><Initials>CI</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>University of Arizona College of Medicine, Phoenix, Arizona, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lewis</LastName><ForeName>Sara A</ForeName><Initials>SA</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>University of Arizona College of Medicine, Phoenix, Arizona, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Galindo</LastName><ForeName>Maureen K</ForeName><Initials>MK</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Banner University Medical Center, Tucson, Arizona, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Cornejo</LastName><ForeName>Patricia</ForeName><Initials>P</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Phoenix Children's, Phoenix, Arizona, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Skidmore</LastName><ForeName>Peter T</ForeName><Initials>PT</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>University of Arizona College of Medicine, Phoenix, Arizona, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Bisarad</LastName><ForeName>Pritha</ForeName><Initials>P</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-2677-9847</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>University of Arizona College of Medicine, Phoenix, Arizona, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Magee</LastName><ForeName>Helen</ForeName><Initials>H</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>University of Arizona College of Medicine, Phoenix, Arizona, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Bontempo</LastName><ForeName>Kelly</ForeName><Initials>K</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Advocate Children's Hospital, Park Ridge, Illinois, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Keren</LastName><ForeName>Boris</ForeName><Initials>B</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>La Piti&#xe9;-Salp&#xea;tri&#xe8;re Hospital, Paris, France.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Afenjar</LastName><ForeName>Alexandra</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>H&#xf4;pital Trousseau, Paris, France.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Skorvanek</LastName><ForeName>Matej</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>University Hospital of L. Pasteur, Kosice, Slovakia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zech</LastName><ForeName>Michael</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-8112-9153</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Technical University of Munich, Munich, Germany.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wentzensen</LastName><ForeName>Ingrid M</ForeName><Initials>IM</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>GeneDx, LLC, Gaithe